  "definitions": {
    "strengthAdjustment": "Y_A = regulation points_A - season own points mean_A - season opponent allowed mean_B + league mean. Y_B symmetric. Var(Y_A-Y_B)=Var(Y_A)+Var(Y_B)-2Cov(Y_A,Y_B). Score covariance uses actual team scores including opposing technical FTs; within-team variance is the game-count-weighted population variance across each team, with per-team values supplied.",
    "pace": "Shared N=(N_A+N_B)/2. Linear score projections on N partition covariance into beta_A*beta_B*Var(N) and residual covariance. Statistical partition, not intervention causality.",
    "iid": "Mean combined regulation possession count times within-team signed possession point variance. Technical opposing points remain signed only for margin IID theory; ordinary offensive point distribution also shown.",
    "serial": "Remainder after IID, marginal pace variance and both covariance components. Contains within-team serial dependence, remaining schedule/lineup heterogeneity and strength fitting effects; cannot identify garbage time causally.",
    "gap": "Components in points squared, not additive SD. Replacing simulation terms with real terms yields the observed variance exactly. Own vs opponent technical bookkeeping is retained, not forced into 0–4."
  },
  "suggestions": [
    "B2 should model state-dependent efficiency/rotation only after matchup and time controls, then validate covariance and residual SD jointly.",
    "Observed >=20-point leads have substantially lower starter exposure in NBA, unlike v1; descriptive association alone does not establish how much variance a substitution rule will remove.",
    "Do not cap game margins or tune wins. Do not infer that all residual covariance is a leader slowdown effect."
  ],
